<p>There are plenty of good reasons a student might want to go to Harvard. One is its long history, at least by American standards; another is the formidable accomplishments of the professors hired to teach there. But on some level, a teenager looking to come up in the world — or, in many cases, parents looking to put their teenager up in the world — understands full well that much of what an institution like Harvard offers comes down to its selectivity. Considering how few get in versus how many apply, it would be easy to believe that most of a student’s battle is won at the point of acceptance. Imagine the despair, then, of a newly arrived Harvardian facing another entrance exam and written application just to get a seat in <a href="https://english.fas.harvard.edu/humanities-10a-humanities-colloquium-homer-morrison">Humanities 10</a>.</p>
<p>Its unintimidating name notwithstanding,&nbsp;Humanities 10 is the opposite of one of those introductory courses through which a vast, darkened lecture hall full of freshmen can doze. “Students read a hefty book a week and write regular essays,”&nbsp;reports Pamela Paul in <a href="https://www.wsj.com/lifestyle/harvard-class-humanities-32e06366?st=JPyU7Y&amp;reflink=desktopwebshare_permalink">a recent <em>Wall Street Journal </em>article. </a><span style="font-size: 13pt;">“There is no pass/fail option. Digital devices are verboten, and attendance is taken in every lecture.” This year’s syllabus, which the </span><em style="font-size: 13pt;">WSJ</em><span style="font-size: 13pt;"> has </span><a style="font-size: 13pt;" href="https://www.wsj.com/arts-culture/books/the-reading-list-for-the-hottest-class-at-harvard-64888ade?st=NbUkGM&amp;reflink=desktopwebshare_permalink">also published</a><span style="font-size: 13pt;"> (also find it below), “begins with </span><em style="font-size: 13pt;">The Odyssey</em><span style="font-size: 13pt;">, travels forward in time to James Joyce’s </span><em style="font-size: 13pt;">Ulysses</em><span style="font-size: 13pt;"> and then backward to end with </span><em style="font-size: 13pt;">The Iliad</em><span style="font-size: 13pt;">. In between are assignments on Montaigne, Shakespeare, Spinoza, Baldwin, Dante and Plato.” Amid these great books of the Western world appear works by Lady Murasaki, Sor Juana, and Cao Xueqin.</span></p>
