<p>With certain exceptions — Baldwin’s essays, three installments of Krzysztof Kieslowski’s <em>Dekalog</em> —&nbsp;this curriculum could be a century old. In fact, Humanities 10 dates back only to 2008,&nbsp;when the English professors (and acclaimed writers) <a href="https://english.fas.harvard.edu/people/stephen-greenblatt">Stephen Greenblatt</a> and <a href="https://english.fas.harvard.edu/people/louis-menand">Louis Menand</a> put it together “as a Harvard twist on a famed Columbia senior seminar and Yale’s Directed Studies program, and a way to combat plummeting student interest in the humanities.” But in a way, its roots run back to 1945,&nbsp;when the university published the report “<a href="https://archive.org/details/generaleducation032440mbp/page/n5/mode/2up">General Education in a Free Society,</a>” which made the case for providing all students with the same non-professional, non-specialized intellectual foundation.&nbsp;Around the same time, it introduced&nbsp;Humanities 2: The Epic and the Novel, a demanding two-semester course that nevertheless drew&nbsp;850 students a year at its peak.</p>
<p>Its successor Humanities 10 accepts only 90 freshmen, and the resulting competition has drawn public attention. So has the admitted students’ willingness to actually do the reading — and their apparent disdain for peers who would look over a few AI summaries and call it an education. In <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SD3AI0r88aA">the video above</a>,&nbsp;<em>Deep Work&nbsp;</em>author <a href="https://calnewport.com/">Cal Newport</a> discusses the truth this reveals, one that recent technological developments have occluded. Relying on shortcuts to feelings of reward — cultivating social media instead of a social life, watching a travel video instead of making a journey, prompting an LLM instead of actually writing — strips our activities of not just their friction, but also their context, and ultimately their meaning. Like any worthwhile human experience, these books don’t hide their value under layers of difficulty; their difficulty is their value.</p>
